        "question": "A 59-year-old man is found to have a 3 x 3 cm enhancing mass in the right kidney on imaging. Hemoglobin is elevated at 17 g/dL. The tumor cells are producing erythropoietin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Renal oncocytoma",
            "B": "Renal cell carcinoma",
            "C": "Angiomyolipoma",
            "D": "Wilms tumor",
            "E": ""
        },
        "correctAnswer": "B",
        "explanation": "Renal Cell Carcinoma. Polygonal clear cells filled with accumulated lipids and carbohydrate. Originates from PCT and invades renal vein. May develop varicocele if left sided. Class triad: flank pain, palpable mass, hematuria. Increase incidence with tobacco smoking and obesity. Associated with paraneoplastic syndromes, e.g., PTHrP, Ectopic EPO, ACTH, Renin (-PEARI-aneoplastic). Clear cell (most common subtype) associated with gene deletion on chromosome 3 (sporadic, or inherited as von Hippel-Lindau syndrome).",
        "hint": "",
        "Point": ""
